<edge source="Positional Encodings" target="Standard Absolute Positional Encodings">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Standard positional encodings face difficulties in handling musical concepts such as key transpositions or repeating rhythm motifs.&lt;SEP&gt;Standard positional encodings rely on absolute positions of notes, which can be problematic for musical motifs.</data>
  <data key="d9">concept,technique</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-140833bd08793c8ef46f4afbc10aa877</data>
  <data key="d11">GA-Unit-4.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1793742990</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Positional Encodings" target="Relative Positional Encodings">
  <data key="d7">4.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Relative Positional Encodings address the shortcomings of standard positional encodings by incorporating distance-based information.&lt;SEP&gt;Relative Positional Encodings are an evolution of Positional Encodings that use distance information to enhance motif preservation.&lt;SEP&gt;Relative Positional Encodings are a variant of Positional Encodings that incorporates distance information between notes.&lt;SEP&gt;Relative Positional Encodings enhance the preservation of musical motifs by accounting for the distance between notes.</data>
  <data key="d9">concept,technique</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-140833bd08793c8ef46f4afbc10aa877</data>
  <data key="d11">GA-Unit-4.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1793742990</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Standard Absolute Positional Encodings" target="Relative Positional Encodings">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Relative Positional Encodings modify standard encodings by considering the distance between notes to preserve motifs across different positions.</data>
  <data key="d9">technique</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-140833bd08793c8ef46f4afbc10aa877</data>
  <data key="d11">GA-Unit-4.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1793742990</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
